CAT = "pbtOrders"; SEG = "ORD"
Q = [
("Where should a phlebotomist check the tube type and handling for an unfamiliar test?",
 "The lab's test directory", ["A coworker's memory", "The patient's chart notes", "The supply catalog"],
 "The laboratory's test directory lists the specimen type, tube, volume and handling for each test it offers."),
("An outpatient arrives for a draw with no order or requisition. What should the phlebotomist do?",
 "Get a valid order first", ["Draw a standard panel", "Draw and wait for an order", "Let the patient pick tests"],
 "Testing needs a valid order from an authorized provider. Without one, the phlebotomist contacts the provider's office before drawing."),
("What is reflex testing?",
 "A test triggered by a result", ["A test repeated every hour", "A test the patient requests", "A test of the patient's reflexes"],
 "Reflex testing runs a follow-up test automatically when the first result meets set criteria, such as a culture after a positive urine screen."),
("A trough level is ordered, but the requisition does not show when the last dose was given. What should the phlebotomist do?",
 "Ask the nurse the dose time", ["Draw it right away", "Guess at the dose schedule", "Skip the draw entirely"],
 "A trough is timed to the next dose, so the phlebotomist confirms the dosing schedule with the nurse before drawing."),
("An unconscious, unidentified trauma patient needs blood work. How is the patient identified?",
 "With a temporary ID number", ["With a written description", "With no ID until awake", "With the room number"],
 "Emergency departments assign a temporary identification number or alias on a band so specimens can be linked until the true identity is known."),
("Which item should never be used to identify a patient?",
 "The room number", ["The full name", "The date of birth", "The medical record number"],
 "Patients change rooms and beds, so a room or bed number is never an identifier. Name, birth date and record number are used."),
("An inpatient's wristband writing has worn off and cannot be read. What should be done?",
 "Have the nurse reband them", ["Draw using memory", "Read the name off the door", "Ask the roommate who it is"],
 "Without a readable band the patient cannot be positively identified, so the nurse rebands the patient before the draw."),
("A transgender patient uses a name that differs from the legal name on the order. What is the best approach?",
 "Use their name, verify ID", ["Use only the legal name aloud", "Refuse to draw the patient", "Change the name on the order"],
 "Addressing patients by the name they use is respectful, while identification still matches the legal identifiers on the order and band."),
("On entering an inpatient room for a draw, what should the phlebotomist do first?",
 "Knock and introduce oneself", ["Turn on all the lights", "Apply the tourniquet", "Open the supply tray at once"],
 "Knocking, introducing oneself and explaining the purpose respects the patient and begins identification."),
("An inpatient is asleep when a routine draw is due. What should the phlebotomist do?",
 "Wake them gently first", ["Draw while they sleep", "Skip the draw entirely", "Shake the bed to wake them"],
 "A patient must be awake to be identified and to agree to the draw, and a startled patient could move and be injured."),
("A physician is examining the patient when the phlebotomist arrives for a routine draw. What is best?",
 "Return when the exam ends", ["Draw during the exam", "Ask the doctor to leave", "Wait inside the exam room"],
 "For a routine draw the phlebotomist comes back later. A STAT draw may be discussed with the provider."),
("An outpatient asks which tests are being drawn today. What is the best response?",
 "Tell them the tests ordered", ["Refuse to say anything", "Tell them it is a secret", "Guess what each one is for"],
 "Patients may know which tests their provider ordered. Interpreting results is left to the provider."),
("If bleeding starts again at home after a draw, what should the patient be told to do?",
 "Press on it, raise the arm", ["Remove the bandage and wash", "Rub the site firmly", "Ignore it until morning"],
 "Firm pressure with the arm raised usually stops renewed bleeding. Bleeding that will not stop needs medical advice."),
("What is a common instruction for the arm after a venipuncture?",
 "Avoid heavy lifting", ["Exercise it hard at once", "Keep it bent tightly", "Soak it in hot water"],
 "Heavy lifting soon after a draw can restart bleeding and cause a bruise."),
("A patient is allergic to chlorhexidine and blood cultures are ordered. What should the phlebotomist do?",
 "Use another approved prep", ["Use chlorhexidine anyway", "Skip all skin cleaning", "Cancel the cultures"],
 "Facilities list an alternative antiseptic, such as an iodine preparation, for patients allergic to chlorhexidine."),
("Why should a patient never be drawn while standing?",
 "They could faint and fall", ["Veins sit too deep then", "The tubes fill far too fast", "The tourniquet slips off"],
 "A patient who faints while standing can be badly hurt. WHO advises a comfortable, preferably lying position."),
("A patient is chewing gum when the phlebotomist arrives. Why ask them to remove it?",
 "It is a choking risk", ["It changes the tube color", "It speeds up clotting", "It ruins the label"],
 "Anything in the mouth is a choking risk if the patient faints during the draw."),
("How should a phlebotomist prepare a patient who is blind for a draw?",
 "Say each step before it happens", ["Begin without speaking", "Speak only to a relative", "Touch first, then explain"],
 "Describing each step before it happens, including the needle, keeps a patient who cannot see from being startled."),
("An older patient with dementia is confused about the draw. What is the best approach?",
 "Stay calm, involve the caregiver", ["Hurry before they object", "Restrain them firmly", "Leave without any attempt"],
 "A calm, simple explanation and help from a caregiver reassure the patient. A patient who refuses is not forced."),
("How should a phlebotomist speak to a patient with an intellectual disability?",
 "Directly, in simple words", ["Only to the caregiver", "Loudly and very quickly", "In technical medical terms"],
 "Speaking directly to the patient with simple words respects them, while a caregiver can help as needed."),
("During a glucose tolerance test, what should the patient do between draws?",
 "Sit quietly, not smoke", ["Walk briskly outside", "Eat a light snack", "Chew gum to pass the time"],
 "Activity, smoking and food all change glucose levels, so the patient rests quietly in the waiting area."),
("How should a phlebotomist treat a comatose patient during a draw?",
 "Talk to them as if they hear", ["Stay silent the whole time", "Skip identification checks", "Draw with no explanation"],
 "Comatose patients may still hear, so the phlebotomist explains what is happening and still checks the ID band."),
("A parent of a young child asks to explain the draw in their own words. What is the best response?",
 "Let them help explain it", ["Insist only staff speak", "Ask them to leave first", "Tell the child nothing at all"],
 "Parents know how their child understands things, and a calm explanation from a parent can ease fear."),
("An outpatient arrives with a trained service dog. What should the phlebotomist do?",
 "Let the dog stay with them", ["Ask them to leave it outside", "Refuse to draw the patient", "Tie the dog in the hallway"],
 "Under the ADA, a service animal may go with the patient into areas open to the public, including a drawing station."),
]
